      "field_name": "publication_type",
      "value": "full_case_report",
      "quote": "Images in Clinical Neurology\nThe Neurohospitalist\n2023, Vol. 13(2) 204 –205\n© The Author(s) 2022\nArticle reuse guidelines:\nsagepub.com/journals-permissions\nDOI: 10. 1177/19418744221133902\njournals.sagepub.com/home/nho\nAnti-Glycine Receptor Antibody-Associated\nLateral Tractopathy\nGabriel E. V ��azquez-V ��elez, MD, PhD 1 ��� and Enmanuel J. Perez, MD, PhD 1 ���\nKeywords\nstiff-person syndrome, autoimmune diseases of the nervous system, myelopathy, lateral tractopathy\nA 53-year-old man with a histo ry of alcohol-use disorder\npresented to the hospital with chronic lower back pain and\nan 18-month history of worsening bilateral lower extremity\nstiffness and gait instability. The patient had been admitted\nmultiple times previously with falls which were attributed\nto alcohol use. The patient ’s exam showed diffuse hypertonia, spasticity and hyperre flexia. MRI of the spinal\ncord showed bilateral lateral tract hyperintensities on STIR\nand T2-weighted images ( Figure 1A and B respectively),\nand hypointensities without e nhancement on T1-weighted\nimages ( Figure 1C ), consistent with a lateral column\nspeci fic process. An extensive diagnostic workup excluded\ntoxic-metabolic, infectious, o r neurodegenerative causes.\nCSF studies were non-in flammatory and CSF antibody\nstudies were negative (includi ng IgG synthesis rate, antiGAD, and anti-glycine recepto r antibodies). Serum paraneoplastic and anti-GAD ant ibodies were negative, but\nserum anti-glycine receptor ( GlyR) antibodies were positive. The patient was diagnosed with anti-GlyR mediated\nautoimmune lateral tractopat hy presenting as stiff person\nsyndrome (SPS).\n1 Anti-GlyR assays are not included in\ncommon send out panels. Anti-GlyR SPS is not commonly\nassociated with spinal cord imaging findings,\n2 however our\nreport includes the first images with findings suggestive of\nan irreversible tract speci fic myelopathy as a result of\nuntreated anti-GlyR SPS. If de tected early, this condition is\ntreatable with immunosu ppressive therapy, 3, 4 otherwise it\ncan lead to irreversible myelomalacia as in this case. Thus,\nit is important that the inpati ent neurologist consider this\ndiagnosis in patients with prog ressive motor symptoms.\nThis case also illustrates the po tential differential sensitivity of serum and CSF antibody assays. Figure 1\nDeclaration of Con flicting Interests\nThe author(s) declared no potential con flicts of interest with respect\nto the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article.",
